course: dictionary
lesson: 13873
title: Kidnap: taking someone against their will
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To kidnap someone means to take them away by force or by tricking them, and keep them somewhere against their will. «The villain planned to kidnap the princess from her castle.» The words against her will tell you the princess did not want to go, so kidnap means taking someone who does not want to be taken.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Police are searching for the boy who was kidnapped from the park.» Police searching and a boy taken from a park show that kidnapped is about a serious, wrong thing done to a person. Try your meaning in the sentence: taken away against his will fits well.
explain: In British English, kidnap can also be a noun, meaning the crime itself. «The kidnap was reported to the police within an hour.» Here kidnap names the crime, not the action. In stories, a kidnap is often the start of an adventure or a mystery.
explain: Keeping safe matters. Stay with a trusted adult, never go off with a stranger, and tell someone straight away if anyone tries to take you away. Your trusted adults always want to know where you are.
fact: Kidnap means to take a person away by force or by tricking them, and keep them against their will.
fact: A kidnapper often wants money, which is called a ransom.
fact: As a verb, the forms are kidnap, kidnaps, kidnapped and kidnapping.
fact: In British English, a kidnap is the crime itself, used as a noun.
fact: Kidnapping is a serious crime, and in law it is punished with long prison sentences.
exercise: «The villain planned to kidnap the princess.» What does kidnap mean here? | To look after a pet | To help someone cross the road | To invite someone to a party | *To take someone away by force or trickery and keep them against their will
exercise: What is the most likely reason a kidnapper takes someone? | To give them a birthday present | To teach them to swim | *To ask for money as a ransom | To help them find lost keys
exercise: If a stranger tries to take you away, what should you do? | Go with them, because they seem nice | Wait and see what happens | Keep it secret and say nothing | *Tell a trusted adult straight away
test: «The kidnap of the famous singer made the news.» Here kidnap is a noun. What does it name? | *The crime of taking someone away against their will | A new song by a famous singer | A news report about the weather | A holiday trip abroad
test: Which word is the -ing form of kidnap? | kidnaps | kidnapped | *kidnapping | kidnapper's
exam: The police arrested the man who tried to ___ the child from the playground. Which word completes the sentence? | *kidnap | forgive | cuddle | celebrate
